# Allowed signers for local-CI receipts (schema simple.local-ci-receipt/v1).
#
# Consumed verbatim by `ssh-keygen -Y verify -f <this file>` from
#   scripts/check/verify-local-ci-receipt.shs
# and, when --allowed-signers is passed, by
#   scripts/check/sign-local-ci-receipt.shs
#
# THIS FILE IS DELIBERATELY EMPTY OF KEYS. That is the fail-closed default: an
# allowlist with no key admits nobody, every receipt verification returns
# non-zero, and CI therefore runs the full gate set. That is the correct
# behaviour until a real key is reviewed and added — a receipt feature that
# starts out trusting somebody is a receipt feature that starts out broken.
# scripts/check/verify-local-ci-receipt.shs pins this: its selftest case (c2)
# fails if this shipped file ever admits a signer.
#
# SECURITY (F6): this file MUST be read from the BASE ref, never from a pull
# request head. A PR that could edit its own allowlist could sign its own
# receipt with its own key and skip the gates it was meant to satisfy. The
# workflow reads it with `pull_request_target` semantics, the same precedent
# `review-admission.yml` already sets.
#
# TRUST CLASS: a key here proves WHO produced a receipt, not that the gates
# truly ran. This is the same trust class as review-admission.yml's
# `self_attestation` input. Add a key only for a signer you would already trust
# to self-attest.
#
# FORMAT — one signer per line, no leading whitespace:
#
#   <principal> namespaces="simple-ci-receipt" <keytype> <base64-key>
#
#   <principal>  the identity the signer passes as --identity, and which the
#                verifier passes to `ssh-keygen -Y verify -I`. Constrained to
#                [A-Za-z0-9._@+-] by both scripts; an email-shaped id is
#                conventional.
#   namespaces=  MUST be exactly "simple-ci-receipt". Without it the key would
#                be accepted for every sshsig namespace, so a signature the
#                signer produced for some unrelated purpose (git commit
#                signing, for instance) could be replayed as a CI receipt.
#   <keytype>    ssh-ed25519 is the expected type; the key material is the
#                whole second and third fields of the signer's .pub file.
#
# TO ADD A REAL KEY
#   1. Generate a key used for NOTHING else:
#          ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C ci-receipt-<who> -f ~/.ssh/simple_ci_receipt
#   2. Append one line, taking the key material verbatim from the .pub file:
#          printf '%s namespaces="simple-ci-receipt" %s\n' \
#              <principal> "$(cat ~/.ssh/simple_ci_receipt.pub | cut -d" " -f1,2)" \
#              >> config/check/ci_receipt_allowed_signers
#   3. Land that line through review on the BASE branch. Adding a key in the
#      same PR whose gates it would let you skip is exactly the attack this
#      file exists to prevent, and reviewers should reject it.
#
# EXAMPLE (commented out on purpose — this is not a usable key):
# ci-receipt@example.invalid namespaces="simple-ci-receipt" ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIExampleExampleExampleExampleExampleExampl
#
# TODO(upgrade path, F4): once a full-CLI pure-Simple binary is deployed to CI,
# add a Simple twin verifier over src/lib/common/crypto/ed25519.spl so the
# receipt check no longer depends on OpenSSH being present on the runner. Do
# not build it before that binary exists — today CI runners have no bin/simple.
ormastes@simple-ci-receipt ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIMqaMD6JgOzm6s/3GECZhmCzY5bi+2djDa7N+cqDGKu0 ormastes@simple-ci-receipt
